[Music]  chapel hill some faculty there at unc  chapel hill are criticizing their own  university after june  journalist nicole hannah jones announced  exclusively right here on cbs this  morning that she is declining a tenured  position there  she told gail yesterday she accepted a  position instead at howard university  after weeks of controversy all that  comes after the unc  board of trustees delayed its decision  to grant her tenure  some faculty at unc's journalism school  wrote a public  letter yesterday saying the way the  university treated her  was racist we spoke to a board member  about all of this and nikki batista is  here with more nikki good morning to you  good morning everyone in that public  letter faculty members also said unc's  treatment of the pulitzer prize-winning  journalist  was humiliating inappropriate and unjust  after hannah jones announcement we took  a look at the fallout for unc  very difficult decision not a decision i  wanted to make  a difficult decision that unc dean susan  king says she also wishes nicole hannah  jones  didn't have to make the head of chapel  hills journalism school was one of  hannah jones fiercest advocates  nicole was going to be what i felt  perhaps my most important legacy last  year hannah jones was appointed as the  night chair of race and investigative  journalism  the controversy began after it became  public  that the position was offered with a  five-year contract  instead of tenure this was a position  that since the 1980s  came with tenure the night chairs are  designed for professional journalists  who have been working in the field to  come into academia and every other chair  before me  who also happened to be white received  that position with tenure  i it has never been denied no one had  never been denied tenure before  exactly after public outcry and student  protests  the board of trustees took up a vote  last week offering hannah jones  their alumni tenure but she ultimately  decided  not to accept it why do you suppose they  didn't  right away offer you tenure what do you  think the reason is  i mean what has been reported is that  there was a great deal of political  interference  by conservatives who don't like the work  that i've done particularly the 1619  project hannah jones 1619 project aimed  to re-examine  american slavery and its impact on  society policies and the legal system  it earned her journalism's highest honor  the pulitzer prize  do you think this had anything to do  with  ms hannah jones 16 19 project i'm sure  it had some impact  the project has long been criticized by  republicans  including former president donald trump  this project  rewrites american history to teach our  children  that we were founded on the principle of  oppression  not freedom nothing could be further  from the truth  there are 13 members of unc's board of  trustees  who voted on hannah jones tenure four of  them are appointed by the  republican-controlled state house and  senate  cbs news spoke to board of trustees  member ralph meekins who says he thinks  it was poor communication not politics  that was to blame for hannah jones not  initially being offered tenure  he said the chair of the committee  reviewing hannah jones tenure  application  had some questions back in january which  was typical in the review process  when he says they didn't hear back  regarding those questions  the process stalled meekins also told us  he doesn't think it was about the  content or  truthfulness of the 1619 project which  he called  impactful work adding he admires hannah  jones and wishes she was coming to unc  hannah jones denies that anyone at unc  requested  additional information for her tenure  application  janae nelson with the naacp legal  defense and educational fund  is hannah jones legal counsel if ms anna  jones went forward with a lawsuit what  would the grounds of the lawsuit  be so there are several but they are  rooted in  violations of the first amendment and  also  race discrimination gender  discrimination and the intersection of  both  there's also a state law in north  carolina that prohibits employment  discrimination  and political influence on state  decisions  that academic freedom on campus has been  in trouble for quite some time  greg lukianov is the president and ceo  of the foundation for individual rights  and education  his organization was one of the first to  criticize hannah jones  not getting tenure my hope is what will  come out of this case  is a greater awareness of the threat to  academic freedom  as for dean king she says she's ready to  help unc  journalism students heal while using  hannah jones as an example  of what they can achieve she is not just  a great journalist  she's a once in a generation journalist  hannah jones told us the university's  provost and chancellor never reached out  during the process  in a statement the chancellor said he is  disappointed that hannah jones will not  be coming to chapel hill  and that he is committed to making unc a  more welcoming  place and you can't help but wonder that  journalism students for years to come  are probably going to be learning about  this situation oh they absolutely will  and i think her  point in that letter is that this isn't  just her fighting for herself but on  behalf of the many people  whose opportunities are stifled because  there are certain people that don't want  certain people in these institutions  and for sure the students at howard  university are lucky they will have her  as a teacher yeah yeah pretty remarkable  a lot of people are also living  vicariously through her  being able to say wow someone said i  don't want this job i got a better job  take that and she had a lot of options  yeah a lot of people are good  all right nicky thank you

